## A. Introduction, problem, objective (0:00–2:30)

**0:00 [SHOW] Slide A1, title**

Good day. We are Hans Matthew Del Mundo, Vin Marcus Gerebise, Gerald Helbiro Jr. and Ira Chloie Narisma, and our assigned algorithm is Simulated Annealing, from the paper "Performance Analysis of Classical Algorithms for the Traveling Salesman Problem" by Luna-Garcia, Candano and Caga-anan. We use it to solve a real routing problem here in Cagayan de Oro City.

**0:15 [SHOW] Slide A2, the problem**

Our scenario is illustrative but realistic. A courier of the Philippine Red Cross Misamis Oriental chapter leaves the chapter office with blood units. The courier must deliver to {{n_hospitals}} hospitals in the city, visit each hospital exactly once, and come back to the office. That is exactly the Traveling Salesperson Problem: one vehicle, a fixed set of stops, a closed tour, and a total length we want as small as possible. A shorter route means the blood arrives sooner and the vehicle uses less fuel.

**1:00 [SHOW] Slide A3, the locations**

These are our {{n}} locations. V0 is the starting point, {{depot_name}}. V1 to V{{n_hospitals}} are the hospitals. The coordinates come from {{coord_source}}, and each place is recorded in our data file with its address and a Google Maps link.

**1:45 [SHOW] Slide A4, objective, assumptions, constraints**

Our objective is measurable: minimise the total driving distance in kilometres. The constraints are that the route starts and ends at the depot, visits every hospital exactly once, and travels on roads only. We assume one vehicle with no capacity limit, we ignore the time spent at each stop, and we use driving distances from {{distance_source_short}}, obtained on {{measured_dates}}. One deliberate change from the paper: the paper uses straight-line distances between points. In a real city, one-way streets and bridges make the road distance very different from the straight line, and sometimes different in each direction. So we use real driving distances.

